{{Politics of the Czech Republic}}

Indirect presidential elections were held in the [[Czech Republic]] in January and February 2003 to elect a new [[President of the Czech Republic|President]]. The [[Parliament of the Czech Republic]] failed to elect a candidate on the first two ballots on the 15 and 24 January. However, on the third round of the third ballot on 28 February, [[Václav Klaus]] was elected.

== Background and procedure ==
{{main|President of the Czech Republic}}
In 2003 [[Václav Havel]] had served the maximum 2 consecutive terms as President of the Czech Republic, with his second term ending on 2 February 2003. A [[joint session]] of the Parliament of the Czech Republic was held on the 15 January 2003 to elect his successor.<ref name="attempt">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71100373.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160307163100/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71100373.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=7 March 2016|title=Czech legislators to make new attempt to choose Havel successor on Jan. 24|date=17 January 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=[[HighBeam Research]]|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=19 June 2012}}</ref>

Before the constitution was amended in 2012 to establish direct presidential election,<ref name="signs">{{cite news|url=http://praguemonitor.com/2012/02/20/klaus-signs-bill-direct-presidential-elections|title=Klaus signs bill on direct presidential elections|date=20 February 2012|work=[[Prague Daily Monitor]]|accessdate=21 June 2012|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://archive.today/20130131194038/http://praguemonitor.com/2012/02/20/klaus-signs-bill-direct-presidential-elections|archivedate=31 January 2013}}</ref> the President of the Czech Republic was elected indirectly by a joint session of the Czech Parliament.<ref name=attempt/> Each ballot had 3 rounds, with a candidate needing an absolute majority of both the 200 members of the [[Chamber of Deputies of the Parliament of the Czech Republic|Chamber of Deputies]] and the 81 members of the [[Senate of the Parliament of the Czech Republic|Senate]] in order to be elected in the first round.<ref name="farewell">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71039728.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160327053544/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71039728.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=27 March 2016|title=Czech Leader Vaclav Havel Gives Farewell|last=Hejma|first=Ondrej|date=15 January 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Online|access-date=19 June 2012}}</ref> When no candidate achieved a majority in both houses of parliament in the first round, then a second round was held between the best-placed candidate in the Chamber vote and the best-placed candidate in the Senate vote, again with an absolute majority needed to get in both houses,<ref name="convenes">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71031371.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160309195656/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71031371.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=9 March 2016|title=Czech Parliament convenes to elect successor to President Vaclav Havel|last=Hejma|first=Ondrej|date=15 January 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=19 June 2012}}</ref> but only among the members who were present at the election.

If the second round was also unsuccessful, then in the third round a candidate needed to win a majority of all present members of parliament, but with votes of both chambers being taken together.<ref name=convenes/> So if all the members of parliament were present a candidate would need 141 votes, from a combination of deputies and senators, in order to be elected in the third round.<ref name="stumbles">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/2690561.stm|title=Czech presidential election stumbles|date=24 January 2003|publisher=[[BBC News Online]]|access-date=21 June 2012}}</ref> When no candidate is elected in all 3 rounds, the process would be repeated at further joint sessions until a candidate was elected.<ref name=stumbles/>

