== First ballot ==
=== Initial candidates ===
The leading party in the government coalition, the [[Czech Social Democratic Party|Social Democratic Party]], nominated a former [[justice minister]] [[Jaroslav Bureš]].<ref name="radio">{{cite news|url=http://www.radio.cz/en/section/news/news-of-radio-prague-668|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130210110349/http://www.radio.cz/en/section/news/news-of-radio-prague-668|url-status=dead|archive-date=10 February 2013|title=News of Radio Prague|date=15 December 2002|publisher=[[Radio Prague]]|accessdate=18 June 2012}}</ref> He defeated a former leader of his party and [[List of Prime Ministers of the Czech Republic|Prime Minister]], [[Miloš Zeman]], for the nomination, despite Zeman winning a [[Czech Social Democratic Party presidential primaries, 2002|party primary]], causing splits in the party.<ref name="step">{{cite news|url=http://www.radio.cz/en/section/curraffrs/-presidential-candidates-step-forward-as-vaclav-havels-term-draws-to-an-end-|title=Presidential candidates step forward as Vaclav Havel's term draws to an end|last=Hrobský|first=Martin|date=18 December 2002|publisher=[[Radio Prague]]|accessdate=18 June 2012}}</ref> Zeman was a rival of the Prime Minister and Social Democrat leader [[Vladimír Špidla]] and Bureš was an attempted compromise candidate for the party.<ref name=stumbles/>

The junior party in the coalition, the [[Christian and Democratic Union – Czechoslovak People's Party|Christian Democratic Party]], meanwhile nominated the chairman of the Senate and former Prime Minister between 1990 and 1992, [[Petr Pithart]] as their candidate.<ref name=radio/><ref name="choose">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-70367070.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304085207/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-70367070.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=4 March 2016|title=Christian Democrats choose presidential candidate|date=14 December 2002|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=18 June 2012}}</ref> He was also supported by the [[Freedom Union – Democratic Union|Freedom Union]] party, another party in the governing coalition.<ref name=step/>

The main opposition party, the [[Civic Democratic Party (Czech Republic)|Civic Democratic Party]], chose the former leader of the party and prime minister, [[Václav Klaus]], as their candidate.<ref name=radio/> Klaus declared his candidacy in October 2002 and stepped down as leader of his party in December, after having led his party to defeat at the [[2002 Czech legislative election|2002 parliamentary election]].<ref name=step/><ref name="former">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-68639463.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160414075744/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-68639463.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=14 April 2016|title=Former prime minister to run for president|date=10 October 2002|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=18 June 2012}}</ref>

The final candidate in the election was [[Miroslav Kříženecký]], a former military prosecutor, who was supported by the [[Communist Party of Bohemia and Moravia|Communist Party]].<ref name=radio/>

=== Voting ===
[[File:Pithart Petr 6168.JPG|250px|thumb|Petr Pithart came second in the first ballot of the election]]
Reports before the election had Petr Pithart and Václav Klaus as the favourites for the election, due to disunity in the Social Democrats, with the Social Democrats and some Communists expected to support Pithart on the second round against Václav Klaus.<ref name="lobby">{{cite news|url=http://www.radio.cz/en/section/news/news-of-radio-prague-693|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130206111050/http://www.radio.cz/en/section/news/news-of-radio-prague-693|url-status=dead|archive-date=6 February 2013|title=News of Radio Prague|last=Cameron|first=Rob|date=14 January 2003|publisher=[[Radio Prague]]|accessdate=18 June 2012}}</ref><ref name="fear">{{cite news|url=https://www.questia.com/read/1G1-96417898/czech-parliament-to-pick-president-some-fear-that|title=Czech parliament to pick president; Some fear that no candidate will win required majority.|last=Konviser|first=Bruce|date=14 January 2003|work=[[The Washington Times]]|publisher=|access-date=|archive-date=2020-09-04|via=[[Questia]]|url-access=subscription|location=Washington, DC|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200904101953/https://www.questia.com/read/1G1-96417898/czech-parliament-to-pick-president-some-fear-that}}</ref> However, there was scepticism that any candidate would be able to be elected in the first ballot on the 15 January 2003.<ref name="successor">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71014028.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160413113809/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71014028.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=13 April 2016|title=Parliament to elect successor to President Vaclav Havel|last=Rybarova|first=Nadia|date=14 January 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=18 June 2012}}</ref> Before the votes the outgoing President Václav Havel delivered a farewell speech for which he received a [[standing ovation]], after urging legislators to follow their conscience.<ref name="inconclusive">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71038073.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160310000142/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71038073.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=10 March 2016|title=Second round in vote to choose Havel successor inconclusive|last=Hejma|first=Ondrej|date=15 January 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=19 June 2012}}</ref>

The first vote saw no candidate elected, but with the Social Democrat candidate, Jaroslav Bureš, and the Communist candidate, Miroslav Kříženecký being eliminated with both receiving 46 votes from deputies and senators.<ref name=attempt/><ref name="results">{{cite journal|last=Linek|first=Lukáš|author2=Mansfeldová, Zdenka|date=6 December 2004|title=Czech Republic|journal=European Journal of Political Research|volume=43|issue=7–8|pages=980–988|doi=10.1111/j.1475-6765.2004.00187.x}}</ref> Almost half of the Social Democrats lawmakers did not vote for Bureš, highlighting the divisions within the party.<ref name=attempt/> Very surprising was low support for Petr Pithart. He received only 20 votes in Chamber of Deputies even though KDU-ČSL that nominated him had 21 Members of the Chamber.<ref name="VOLBA PREZIDENTA REPUBLIKY V ČESKÉ REPUBLICE" />

Václav Klaus and Petr Pithart competed in the next two rounds but neither was able to be elected,<ref name="fail">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71074780.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130125030954/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71074780.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=25 January 2013|title=Czechs fail to choose a successor for Havel|date=16 January 2003|work=[[The Record (Bergen County)|The Record]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|access-date=19 June 2012}}</ref> with Klaus getting the most votes in the Chamber of Deputies, while Pethart received the most votes in the Senate in all three election rounds.<ref name="china">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-8800277.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160312094734/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-8800277.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=12 March 2016|title=Czech's parliament fails to elect president|date=17 January 2003|work=[[China Daily]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|access-date=19 June 2012}}</ref> Klaus led throughout and in the third round received 113 votes to 89 for Pithart,<ref name=results/> but as 84 legislators submitted empty ballots neither was able to reach the required 141 votes.<ref name=china/> The first ballot showed disunity of ČSSD during the election. It also showed that Klaus has much higher support than it was expected.<ref name="VOLBA PREZIDENTA REPUBLIKY V ČESKÉ REPUBLICE" />

