== Between the second and third ballots ==
The presidential term of Václav Havel finished on the 2 February, which meant that legislators had 30 days to elect a successor according to the [[Constitution of the Czech Republic|constitution]].<ref name="singer">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71525864.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130125032455/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71525864.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=25 January 2013|title=Popular Czech singer is considering running for president|date=5 February 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]]|via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref> During the period in which there was no president, the powers of the president were assumed jointly by the Prime Minister Vladimír Špidla and the [[Speaker (politics)|Speaker]] of the Chamber of Deputies [[Lubomír Zaorálek]].<ref name="without">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71463365.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160409185008/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71463365.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=9 April 2016|title=Czech Republic is without a president for the first time|last=Rybarova|first=Nadia|date=3 February 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref> This was the first time the Czech Republic had been without a president.<ref name=without/>

[[Opinion poll]]s showed the population strongly supported changing the constitution to allow the direct election of the president by popular vote, with political leaders indicating they would attempt to make this change if a third attempt to elect a president was unsuccessful.<ref name=singer/> On the 5 February a popular Czech singer, [[Karel Gott]], indicated he would be interested in standing if direct popular election was introduced.<ref name=singer/>

Following his defeat in the first round of the second ballot Social Democrat Milos Zeman announced he would not run in a third ballot.<ref name=agree/> However the leader of the opposition Civic Democrats, [[Mirek Topolánek]], restated his party's support for Václav Klaus on the 3 February and called for a new election date to be set.<ref name="demands">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-97254760.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924165639/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-97254760.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=24 September 2015|title=Czech Republic: Czech opposition party demands date for presidential election.|date=5 February 2003|work=Info Prod Research |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|location=Middle East|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref> The Speaker Lubomír Zaorálek said on the 7 February that the third ballot would be held on the 28 February.<ref name="third">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71564603.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924181742/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71564603.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=24 September 2015|title=Lawmakers will make third attempt to choose Havel successor|date=7 February 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref>

== Third ballot ==
[[File:Vaclav Klaus headshot.jpg|thumb|Václav Klaus was elected on the third vote of the third ballot]]
Since the failed second ballot the 3 parties in the governing coalition, the Social Democrats, Christian Democrats and Freedom Union, had held talks in an attempt to agree on a candidate.<ref name="replace">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71873776.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160307214108/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-71873776.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=7 March 2016|title=Ruling coalition names presidential candidate to replace Vaclav Havel|date=19 February 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref> On the 19 February 2003 the 3 parties nominated [[Jan Sokol (philosopher)|Jan Sokol]], the [[Dean (education)|Dean]] of [[Charles University in Prague]], who formerly had for a short time been [[education minister]] in a [[caretaker government]].<ref name=replace/> Sokol had been unanimously backed by the 63 Social Democratic deputies present at a party meeting earlier and if all the legislators from the governing coalition backed Sokol they would be able to elect him as president.<ref name=replace/> By the 25 February a total of 97 legislators from the governing coalition in the Chamber of Deputies signed a declaration to officially nominate Sokol.<ref name="fields">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72065205.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160314070142/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72065205.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=14 March 2016|title=Ruling coalition fields joint candidate in third attempt to replace Havel|last=Janicek|first=Karel|date=25 February 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref>

At the third ballot on the 28 February 2003, Václav Klaus led on the first 2 votes against Jan Sokol, but failed to reach the necessary majority, as deputies voted in favour of Klaus, but senators favored Sokol.<ref name="elected">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72142930.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160319214739/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72142930.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=19 March 2016|title=Vaclav Klaus Is Elected Czech President|last=Janicek|first=Karel|date=28 February 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Online|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref> However, on the third vote Klaus won the support of 142 legislators, narrowly achieving the necessary 141 votes to defeat Sokol who won 124 votes, while 14 legislators abstained and one was absent.<ref name=elected/> Decisive was the support of members of the Communist Party and of a group of rebels from the governing coalition that refused to support Sokol.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.radio.cz/en/section/news/news-of-radio-prague-740|title=News|publisher=Radio Prague|access-date=16 August 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120924062357/http://www.radio.cz/en/section/news/news-of-radio-prague-740|archive-date=24 September 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>  Klaus was sworn in as president on the 8 March.<ref name="over">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-1751024.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130125130924/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-1751024.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=25 January 2013|title=Klaus takes over from Vaclav Havel|date=8 March 2003|work=[[The Independent]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref>

