==Aftermath==
After the election the Prime Minister Vladimír Špidla called a vote of confidence, as the coalition had failed to elect the candidate they had backed.<ref name="survives">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72476349.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160325040329/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72476349.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=25 March 2016|title=Czech government survives vote of confidence|last=Rybarova|first=Nadia|date=11 March 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref> This showed that some legislators from the coalition must have voted for Klaus, however the government won a vote of confidence on the 11 March after all 101 government deputies voted in favour in the 200 member Chamber of Deputies, while the whole opposition voted against.<ref name=survives/> Spidla then fired the minister of trade and industry, Jiri Rusnok, due to "personal differences", but Rusnok said he thought it was because he had backed Klaus in the presidential vote.<ref name="fires">{{cite news|url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72532183.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160308233816/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-72532183.html|url-status=usurped|archive-date=8 March 2016|title=Czech premier fires minister of trade and industry|last=Janicek|first=Karel|date=13 March 2003|agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=HighBeam Research|url-access=subscription|work=AP Worldstream|access-date=20 June 2012}}</ref>

Klaus was [[2008 Czech presidential election|elected]] for second term in 2008 and left the office in 2013 when he wasn't eligible to reelection. He endorsed his former rival [[Miloš Zeman]] during [[2013 Czech presidential election|2013 presidential election]].<ref name="Klaus podpořil Miloše Zemana na Hrad">{{cite web|title=Klaus podpořil Miloše Zemana na Hrad|url=https://www.novinky.cz/domaci/280725-klaus-podporil-milose-zemana-na-hrad.html|website=Novinky.cz|accessdate=22 May 2017|language=cs}}</ref> Zeman previously endorsed Klaus during 2008 presidential election.<ref name="Zeman chce na Hradě dál Klause">{{cite web|title=Zeman chce na Hradě dál Klause|url=https://zpravy.aktualne.cz/domaci/politika/zeman-chce-na-hrade-dal-klause/r~i:article:506903/|website=Aktuálně.cz|accessdate=22 May 2017|language=cs|date=18 September 2007}}</ref>

Zeman's defeat is considered to have strong impact on Czech politics. It is stated that he distanced himself from ČSSD since the election.<ref name="Zrádce omlouvá vlastní zradu, vrací Zeman úder Sobotkovi">{{cite magazine |title=Zrádce omlouvá vlastní zradu, vrací Zeman úder Sobotkovi|url=http://www.tyden.cz/rubriky/domaci/politika/zradce-omlouva-vlastni-zradu-vraci-zeman-uder-sobotkovi_335641.html |magazine=[[Týden]]|accessdate=22 May 2017|date=8 March 2015 |language=cs}}</ref> He later left the party and founded [[Party of Civic Rights]] (SPO). He became SPO's presidential candidate in [[2013 Czech presidential election|2013 presidential election]] and became first directly elected Czech president. His presidency is influenced by his conflicts with leadership of ČSSD.<ref name="Zrádce omlouvá vlastní zradu, vrací Zeman úder Sobotkovi" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Zeman a Babiš jsou na stejné vlně, škodí ČSSD, míní Chovanec|url=http://echo24.cz/a/wzpXu/zeman-a-babis-jsou-na-stejne-vlne-skodi-cssd-mini-chovanec|website=Echo24|accessdate=22 May 2017|language=cs|date=5 November 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=ČSSD uvažuje o vlastním kandidátovi na Hrad|url=https://www.novinky.cz/domaci/437929-cssd-uvazuje-o-vlastnim-kandidatovi-na-hrad.html|website=Novinky.cz|accessdate=22 May 2017|language=cs}}</ref>

